winebus.sys: Decode an Xbox controller that macOS passes through as GIP.

On macOS 15 and later an Xbox controller on USB is claimed by Apple's
XboxUSBDevice driver. It reaches IOHID as a device whose primary usage says
gamepad but whose report descriptor holds nothing but vendor-defined bytes:
the controller's own GIP packets, passed through. winebus admits it as a raw
gamepad, and winexinput.sys then has nothing to read ("HidP_GetButtonCaps
returned 0xc0110004"), so XInput reports no controller at all. The synthetic
HID gamepad macOS also publishes for it (AppleGCSyntheticDevice, 045e:028e)
is not visible to IOHIDManager, so it is no way round. CrossOver's own Xbox
bus speaks the Xbox 360 protocol over IOUSB and disables itself on Sequoia.

Report 0x20 is GIP's input packet: a four-byte header, two button bytes,
two 10-bit triggers, then four signed 16-bit stick axes with up as positive.
Report 0x07 carries the guide button. When a Microsoft device's descriptor
has vendor pages and no buttons or axes, build the gamepad the SDL backend
builds and translate these reports into it, with SDL's button indices. The
device stays flagged hidraw: it is the same raw device, decoded, and the same
prefix settings that admit a Bluetooth gamepad admit this one.

Measured on a 045e:0b12 under Wine 11.0 from crossover-sources-26.3.0 on
macOS 26: reports every 8 ms, XInput's state 1.2 ms (median) behind them,
every stick value identical. Rumble is not implemented. See
docs/controllers.md.

--- a/dlls/winebus.sys/bus_iohid.c
+++ b/dlls/winebus.sys/bus_iohid.c
@@ -265,8 +265,174 @@
     iohid_device_set_output_report,
     iohid_device_get_feature_report,
     iohid_device_set_feature_report,
+};
